cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:44:- **Checks on this version (CPU only).** `tests/kaggriculture/test_configs.py` derives every per-rank shape from `scaling_6m` divided by the world size and fails loudly when a global quantity, a per-rank minibatch or a teacher chunk is not a whole division (world sizes 3 and 32, accumulation 3 and teacher chunk 24 are rejected). It asserts the 8-rank global workload equals `scaling_6m`, and that the startup GEMM check sees 64 rollout, 256 minibatch, 4,096 teacher-chunk and 64 evaluation rows, one trunk and one head call each. `tests/scripts/test_run_ppo.py` drives `run_ppo.main` from the 8-rank YAML to the headroom lines and the explicit not-wired stop. Changing the YAML to spm 4 turned four config tests red and restoring it byte-for-byte turned them green (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/8rank/mutation-spm4.log`). `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 uvx --from rust-just just py-prepare` passed: ruff, format, mypy (62 files), 1,534 passed and 5 skipped (native grammar binding, two FlashAttention/CUDA, quantized backend, native Kaggriculture env), docs-fresh (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/8rank/py-prepare.log`). No GPU, Rust or training check ran.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:45:- **Component estimate, not end-to-end.** The GPU checks bundle ("GPU checks bundle (component)" in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/results.md` on branch `kg/rebuild-gpu-checks`, ops evidence pending merge into this branch) timed the model alone at the 8-rank per-rank shape: 1.389 s (mid) and 2.372 s (dense) per update, or 11,796 and 6,906 global env steps/s if ranks scaled perfectly. That is 0.90 (mid) and 0.95 (dense) of four times the 2-rank rate. The smaller train-step batch is the common cost: at mid the rollout forwards and train steps add about equally (+0.295 s and +0.300 s), while the dense loss is train-step dominated (+0.572 s, rollout −0.063 s). It excludes the engine, host copies, GAE, logging and all-reduce. The timings ran alone on one GPU (GPU 0) of the two-GPU pod, with synthetic observations and fresh weights (the bundle as a whole used GPUs 0 and 1). It predicts no end-to-end SPS.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:46:- **Open measurements (Task 6.3b).** The 8-GPU pod is a new billable resource: read and state its live price and get the owner's approval before creating it. Then measure, at 8-rank shapes: dense-state memory against the 85% target; complete-work SPS in env steps and learner turns, with phase costs; gradient all-reduce cost on the pod's actual interconnect (RTX PRO 6000 has no NVLink; read the topology rather than assume PCIe behavior); vCPUs per GPU against the Rust engine's step time and `native_threads`; the first-minibatch log-ratio alarm on every rank; W&B live sync; and seed-stream disjointness at world size 8 (the Task 1.4 L3 test includes world size 8).
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:50:  - **(c2) Batch shape only:** 512 envs × horizon 128, spm 32 (64 envs/rank, spm 4 at 8 ranks) on `scaling_6m` settings. This matches neither Isaiah recipe: each of the 16 optimizer steps per iteration sees 4,096 transitions instead of 1,024 (65,536 per iteration instead of 16,384) under `scaling_6m`'s step-defined schedule. It is a recipe deviation, so adopting it requires a measured comparison against the divided `scaling_6m` shape (complete-work throughput and learning at equal env steps), recorded in the [[recipe-choices-align-to-isaiah-without-owner-escalation|alignment Decision]]. Low GPU utilization at 32 envs/rank is the trigger to measure, not a reason to switch.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:9:- [[start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks|Start multi-GPU qualification with two ranks]] — Qualify on two GPUs before four; 2/4-rank RTX PRO 6000 verification is authorized; keep the pod running after bounded runs; host history lives in ops. Owner 2026-09-29: eight RTX PRO 6000 likely for the main run, two ranks for diagnosis and building; the Isaiah-divided 8-rank config (32 envs/rank, spm 2) is CPU-tested, and its qualification (Task 6.3b) awaits an owner-approved 8-GPU pod. Isaiah's only 8-GPU config (scaling_50m) keeps 256 envs/rank while his 6M precedent divides; winner_ce_6m is not an aligned fallback (full recipe: owner reward decision; batch shape alone: measured deviation).
cookbook/decisions/v3-reuses-isaiah-infrastructure-with-kaggriculture-semantics.md:26:Reuse the **starter transformer infrastructure with newly written Kaggriculture stems and action heads**. Do not import v2's model implementation, encoder, attention stack or trainer. V2 game-engine semantics, feature coordinate meanings, action grammar/codec contracts and selected reward formulas are permitted source material. The [[../references/explicit-game-tokens-and-grammar-replace-orbit-heads|model record]] names the implementation boundary; the [[../references/reward-reuse-preserves-objective-and-critic-semantics|reward record]] distinguishes exact v2 formulas from a newly selected v3 recipe. No v2 reward coefficient becomes an optimal v3 default merely because the formula can be reused. The owner reiterates that most of the model architecture should come from Isaiah and that training performance problems must be addressed. Accordingly retain the starter transformer/attention/training mechanisms, justify game-specific replacements, and measure actual bottlenecks rather than claiming performance from ancestry. This does not import the v2 neural implementation.
cookbook/decisions/throughput-means-correct-complete-work.md:22:The v2 native-sampler Reference is useful contrary evidence: its saved shared-host run lowered sampler CPU time while complete collection elapsed time increased. That is a scoped counterexample to extrapolating component gains, not a ban on native sampling and not a v3 benchmark. Reducing environments, histories or data volume is a workload change unless comparable end-to-end measurements show an optimization.
cookbook/decisions/throughput-means-correct-complete-work.md:24:Use [[../workflows/profile-cuda-bottlenecks-with-nsight-systems|Nsight Systems]] for NVIDIA timeline attribution. Temporary downscaling can diagnose a fault but does not close the throughput requirement. Historical measured scopes (the rejected old decoder and the fixed-minibatch sweep) live on branch `kg/reference-2026-09-29` at `ops/gpu-sps-2026-09-29/results.md`, not in the current tree. The rebuild so far has only component measurements, recorded as the [[../references/model-only-sps-ceiling-bounds-per-rank-throughput|model-only SPS ceiling]], an upper bound rather than a throughput result. None of them establishes playing strength, dense-game capacity or an owner-specified performance floor, and each recipe change, including the Isaiah-aligned cadence, needs its own complete-work qualification.
cookbook/references/index.md:9:- [[kaggriculture-configs-follow-isaiahs-scaling-6m-recipe|Kaggriculture configs follow Isaiah's scaling_6m recipe]] — Task 3.4: 2-rank 128/8, 4-rank 64/4 and 8-rank 32/2 configs deliberately keep scaling_6m's global batch, optimizer, PPO, teacher and compile settings; they load through the real FullConfig with a typed KaggricultureEnvConfig; run_ppo startup checks every forward against the model's GEMM chunking before allocation, prints bounded headroom, then stops explicitly because no Kaggriculture env is wired yet.
