cookbook/decisions/recipe-choices-align-to-isaiah-without-owner-escalation.md:32:1. **Optimizer-step cadence.** Apply Isaiah's 6M multi-GPU division from `winner_ce_6m_4x5090.yaml` (his per-rank fit of `winner_ce_6m`): divide `n_envs` and `segments_per_minibatch` by world size, keeping the global batch, 16 optimizer steps/iteration and env steps/iteration of the single-GPU run. His larger scaling configs keep 256 envs/rank instead (his only 8-GPU config, `scaling_50m`, grows the global batch with ranks), so the division is his 6M precedent, not a universal rule. `winner_ce_6m` itself is not an alignment fallback: its full recipe (win-only reward, winner cross-entropy critic, its own LR schedule and weight decay) is an owner reward-design decision, and its 512 × 128 batch shape alone on `scaling_6m` settings is a recipe deviation that needs a measured comparison ([[start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks|multi-GPU Decision]], variants c1/c2). For `scaling_6m` (256 envs, spm 16, accumulation 1) this gives 128 envs/rank, spm 8, accumulation 1 on two ranks, and 64/4/1 on four ranks. `target_kl` stays null as upstream. Each iteration then collects 16,384 game transitions and takes 16 optimizer steps of 1,024 global transitions, matching upstream warm-up/cosine data exposure. Residual differences: two-player games only, and dense-game memory is unmeasured, so a memory smoke precedes any long run. Earlier 4,096-env SPS figures do not qualify this cadence.
